                      (    forth6809.asm):09134         
                      (    forth6809.asm):09135                    ; ALGORITHM REPLACED (caught by MAME testing after the
                      (    forth6809.asm):09136                    ; argument-order fix above): the entire body below used
                      (    forth6809.asm):09137                    ; to decode backslash escape sequences (\n, \t, \\, \") -
                      (    forth6809.asm):09138                    ; a plausible-looking but entirely wrong reading of what
                      (    forth6809.asm):09139                    ; ANS UNESCAPE does. Confirmed against the actual spec and
                      (    forth6809.asm):09140                    ; its canonical reference implementation (forth-standard.
                      (    forth6809.asm):09141                    ; org/standard/string/UNESCAPE and complang.tuwien.ac.at's
                      (    forth6809.asm):09142                    ; ANS Forth reference text): UNESCAPE has nothing to do
                      (    forth6809.asm):09143                    ; with backslash sequences at all - it replaces every '%'
                      (    forth6809.asm):09144                    ; character with two '%' characters, and nothing else, so
                      (    forth6809.asm):09145                    ; that a literal '%' in text survives an eventual
                      (    forth6809.asm):09146                    ; SUBSTITUTE pass unchanged ("If you pass a string through
                      (    forth6809.asm):09147                    ; UNESCAPE and then SUBSTITUTE, you get the original
                      (    forth6809.asm):09148                    ; string"). Every character that ISN'T '%' passes straight
